---
import Main from "@/layouts/Main.astro";
import PageHeader from "@/components/PageHeader.astro";
import StatCounter from "@/components/StatCounter.astro";
import Placeholder from "@/components/Placeholder.astro";

const investment = [
  { nation: "United States", amount: "$280B", instrument: "CHIPS and Science Act", year: "2022" },
  { nation: "European Union", amount: "Multi-billion EUR", instrument: "EuroHPC Joint Undertaking", year: "2018-" },
  { nation: "United Kingdom", amount: "£1B", instrument: "AI infrastructure scheme", year: "2024" },
  { nation: "India", amount: "$1B", instrument: "IndiaAI", year: "2024" },
  { nation: "Canada", amount: "$2.7B", instrument: "AI Compute Access Fund + SCIP", year: "2024-26" },
];

const energy = [
  { province: "Quebec", cost: "$0.05/kWh", note: "Industrial; among the cheapest globally" },
  { province: "Manitoba", cost: "$0.102/kWh", note: "Second cheapest; hydro-dominant" },
  { province: "British Columbia", cost: "$0.114/kWh", note: "Hydro-dominant" },
  { province: "Alberta", cost: "$0.258/kWh", note: "Deregulated; solar PPA below $30/MWh" },
];
---

<Main
  title="Sovereignty: The problem: Canada's dependence on US infrastructure"
  description="Even data centres in Ontario and Quebec are American-owned and exposed to the US CLOUD Act. Every Canadian AI query runs through American jurisdiction. The problem DAI Compute exists to solve."
  activePath="/sovereignty"
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="The problem we solve"
    title="Canada runs its AI on infrastructure it does not own or govern."
    lede="Canada is structurally dependent on foreign-operated infrastructure for much of its AI economy. Even data centres on Canadian soil can raise control and jurisdiction questions when the operating entity is U.S.-linked. Under the U.S. CLOUD Act, U.S. legal process may be relevant where a provider has possession, custody, or control of data. This is the dependence DAI Compute exists to reduce."
  />

  <!-- Problem 1: the CLOUD Act / ownership trap -->
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 py-20 lg:px-10">
    <p class="field-label" style="color: var(--color-flag-red);">The core problem &middot; Foreign jurisdiction</p>
    <h2 class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-display text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-tight text-snow">
      A Canadian postal code is not Canadian jurisdiction.
    </h2>
    <div class="mt-6 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate">
        <p>
          The largest data centres in Ontario and Quebec are owned and operated
          by American corporations. Because the operating entity is American,
          the U.S. CLOUD Act (2018) lets U.S. authorities compel disclosure of
          the data on those servers, regardless of the fact that the building
          sits on Canadian soil.
        </p>
        <p>
          So every time a Canadian firm runs an AI query, trains a model, or
          stores data through that infrastructure, it is running through
          American legal jurisdiction. Canadian privacy law does not protect
          against it. Geographic location is not the same thing as legal
          sovereignty.
        </p>
        <p class="text-mist">
          The consequence is quiet but compounding: Canadian firms lose
          government contracts on data-residency grounds, Canadian energy is
          converted to foreign profit, and the country's AI economy is built on
          rails it does not control.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex flex-col justify-center border border-hairline bg-ink-2/50 p-8">
        <p class="field-label text-slate">What the CLOUD Act means in practice</p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate">
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span aria-hidden="true" style="color: var(--color-flag-red);">&bull;</span> U.S. authorities can compel U.S.-based providers to disclose data stored anywhere in the world.</li>
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span aria-hidden="true" style="color: var(--color-flag-red);">&bull;</span> A "Canadian region" of a U.S. cloud is marketing, not jurisdiction.</li>
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span aria-hidden="true" style="color: var(--color-flag-red);">&bull;</span> Canadian firms bidding on government work can be disqualified on data-residency grounds.</li>
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span aria-hidden="true" style="color: var(--color-flag-red);">&bull;</span> The exposure grows as AI is embedded in more corporate, health, and government processes.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="field-note mt-5 text-slate/60">
          Confirmed by academic legal analysis (Olorunlana 2025; Spartak 2025).
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Problem 2: the sovereignty investment gap -->
  <section class="border-y border-hairline bg-ink-2/30">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 py-20 lg:px-10">
      <p class="field-label" style="color: var(--color-flag-red);">The scale &middot; A dependence gap</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-display text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-tight text-snow">
        Canada has no system in the global supercomputing elite, and a fraction of the investment.
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-6 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate">
          <p>
            Canada does not appear in the TOP500 top 10 (November 2024). The
            federal government spent $705M on one centralized supercomputer for
            academic researchers, which does not serve the 140,000 professionals
            in industry, the startups, or the citizens.
          </p>
          <blockquote class="border-l-2 pl-5 text-mist" style="border-color: var(--color-flag-red);">
            &ldquo;Currently, most compute capacity is located in other
            countries. This exposes Canadian firms to a reliance on
            privately-owned computing, outside of Canada. This comes with
            dependencies and security risks.&rdquo;
            <cite class="field-note mt-3 block not-italic text-slate">Budget 2024, Chapter 4 (verbatim)</cite>
          </blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="border border-hairline bg-ink-2/60 p-7">
          <p class="field-label text-slate">Sovereign compute investment</p>
          <table class="mt-4 w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-hairline text-left">
                <th class="pb-2 font-medium text-mist">Nation</th>
                <th class="pb-2 text-right font-medium text-mist">Committed</th>
                <th class="pb-2 text-right font-medium text-mist">Year</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {
                investment.map((r) => (
                  <tr class="border-b border-hairline/60">
                    <td class="py-2.5">
                      <span class="font-medium text-snow">{r.nation}</span>
                      <span class="field-note block text-slate/60">{r.instrument}</span>
                    </td>
                    <td class="py-2.5 text-right font-medium" style="color: var(--color-orange);">{r.amount}</td>
                    <td class="field-note py-2.5 text-right text-slate">{r.year}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="field-note mt-4 text-slate/60">Canada's total is roughly 1% of US investment.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Problem 3: the energy paradox -->
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 py-20 lg:px-10">
    <div class="grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <p class="field-label" style="color: var(--color-flag-red);">The paradox &middot; Wasted advantage</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 font-display text-2xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-tight text-snow sm:text-3xl">
          The cheapest clean energy in the G7 powers foreign-owned data centres.
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate">
          Canada has the energy advantage and the cold-climate cooling advantage
          that AI compute needs. Today that advantage is captured by American
          hyperscalers, for their shareholders. The electrons are Canadian; the
          value, the jurisdiction, and the ownership are not.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate">
          The assets exist. What is missing is the mechanism for Canadians to
          finance and own the compute those electrons could power.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-hairline bg-ink-2/60 p-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <tbody>
            {
              energy.map((e, i) => (
                <tr class={i > 0 ? "border-t border-hairline" : ""}>
                  <td class="py-3 align-top">
                    <span class="font-medium text-snow">{e.province}</span>
                    <span class="field-note block text-slate/60">{e.note}</span>
                  </td>
                  <td class="py-3 text-right align-top font-medium" style="color: var(--color-yellow);">{e.cost}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="field-note mt-4 text-slate/60">
          Cold-climate cooling cuts data-centre cooling costs 40-60%. Quebec has 700MW of surplus hydro.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The resolution -->
  <section class="hero-glow relative overflow-hidden border-t border-hairline bg-ink-2/40">
    <div class="tech-grid absolute inset-0 opacity-70"></div>
    <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 py-20 lg:px-10">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <p class="field-label" style="color: var(--color-orange);">The response</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 font-display text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-tight text-snow">
          Let Canadians finance and own the infrastructure themselves.
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-relaxed text-mist">
          DAI Compute is a credit protocol that connects Canadian capital to
          Canadian energy and compute, on Canadian soil, under Canadian law.
          Depositors earn yield from building it; builders borrow against it.
          Sovereignty is the collateral and the moat, the one thing no foreign
          cloud and no global compute market can offer.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-5">
          <a href="/protocol" class="btn-primary inline-flex items-center px-7 py-3.5 text-sm">How the protocol works</a>
          <a href="/future" class="btn-ghost inline-flex items-center px-7 py-3.5 text-sm">The fifty-year vision &rarr;</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Evidence note -->
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 py-16 lg:px-10">
    <div class="max-w-3xl border-l-2 pl-6" style="border-color: var(--color-orange);">
      <p class="field-label text-slate">On the numbers</p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate">
        Every figure on this page traces to a primary government, institutional,
        or peer-reviewed source. Where a commonly cited statistic could not be
        verified against a primary source, we have left it off rather than
        repeat it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Main>
